  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 152 PM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nNot too much has changed in the longterm from yesterday's \ndiscussion. Overall pattern sets up with the area east of the high \npressure ridge over the southern Plains and the trough over the \nnortheast coast. This will put the area in a more \nnorthwest/northerly flow regime into the start of the week. This \npattern change will give us relief from the tropical airmass over \nthe area and widespread showers. Monday morning (~12z) catches just \nthe tail end of the CAMs and HREF which gives more confidence in the \npotential for a vort max to push around the ridge and into northern \nGeorgia. This is showing up as the potential for organized showers \nas it enters northern Georgia. Timing will limit the threat for \nsevere for this round. Tuesday afternoon also showcases another \npotential window for organized convection that you can see the \nemphasis for on the Euro currently. The drier air may be the \nlimiting factor but still could see thunderstorms into the afternoon \nand evening over north Georgia if organized convection is able to \nhold together. \n\nAs you push into the Wednesday/Thursday timeframe, the ridge to the \nwest begins to breakdown with the troughing over the east coast \npushing southward and deepening. This pattern would be conducive of \nhigher rain chances through the weekend but the models do seem a bit \naggressive at this time so will see how this pans out.\n\nWith the ridge to our west and pushing into our area temperatures \ninto Tuesday and Wednesday will increase into the mid to upper 90s \nacross the area with heat indices at widespread 103-107 across the \narea. Heat advisories will likely be needed during this time period.",
